  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are finite sets. Are the following statements true or false? Explain your answers.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p14-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$|A\\cup\\varnothing|=|A|$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. $A\\cup\\varnothing=A$, so their numbers of elements are equal.",
          "worked": "The empty set contributes no elements to the union, so $A\\cup\\varnothing=A$. Therefore $|A\\cup\\varnothing|=|A|$, and the statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$|A\\cap\\varnothing|=0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True. $A\\cap\\varnothing=\\varnothing$, which has no elements.",
          "worked": "No element belongs to the empty set, so there are no elements common to $A$ and $\\varnothing$. Thus $A\\cap\\varnothing=\\varnothing$ and $|A\\cap\\varnothing|=0$. The statement is true.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$|A\\cup B|=|A|+|B|$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. For example, if $A=B=\\{1\\}$, then $|A\\cup B|=1$ but $|A|+|B|=2$.",
          "worked": "Take $A=B=\\{1\\}$. Then $A\\cup B=\\{1\\}$, so $|A\\cup B|=1$, whereas $|A|+|B|=1+1=2$. Since $1\\ne2$, the statement is false. Adding the two set sizes counts their common element twice.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p14-q9-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$|A\\cap B|=|A|-|B|$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. For example, if $A=B=\\{1\\}$, then $|A\\cap B|=1$ but $|A|-|B|=0$.",
          "worked": "Take $A=B=\\{1\\}$. Then $A\\cap B=\\{1\\}$, so $|A\\cap B|=1$, whereas $|A|-|B|=1-1=0$. Since $1\\ne0$, this counterexample shows that the statement is false.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      }
    ]
  },
